\begin{abstract}
Let $P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$ be a Witten operator on a closed connected Riemannian manifold, acting in $L^2(e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$. The spectrum of $P_\alpha$ does not determine $(g,\alpha)$. The full multiplication table of eigenfunctions does. This paper isolates a finite piece of that table.

Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$ and let $\mathcal{V}$ be the sum of eigenspaces of eigenvalue at most $\Lambda$. The visible packet is the restriction of the triple products $\Phi$ to pairs from $\mathcal{V}$ against modes up to $\Lambda'$, together with the weighted tail
$$R(u,v)=-P(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)
=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda$$
and its uniform size $\Delta=\sup\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2$ over unit $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. No Weyl law, no potential, and no curvature bound is used. A Wyman bound on window triples guarantees that the coefficients of $R$ exist.

If $R=0$, the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. If $\Delta$ is smaller than a threshold depending on the embedding of $X$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$, the packet determines a submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ on which
$$\bigl\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
+\bigl\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)},$$
with $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$. When the tail of $\Phi$ occupies a finite slot, Sobolev embedding on $\Sigma$ converts this to a linear bound in $\Delta$ with constants depending only on window dimensions and $k$.

The unweighted first window on the sphere is the minimal finite-tail case: $\Lambda=n$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, and $R=0$ already at $\Lambda'=2n+2$. That frame recognizes the unit round sphere exactly, and recognizes it with linear $C^0$ stability in the frame deficits when $R$ is small. Those statements are frame-side. They are not Obata theorems.

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} sphere, spectral window, triple products, Witten Operators, eigenfunctions, inverse problem
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

A Witten operator on a closed Riemannian manifold $(X,g)$ is the weighted Laplacian
$$P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$$
acting in $L^2(e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$ \cite{Wit82}. Its spectrum is discrete, nonnegative, and unbounded, with kernel the constant functions. The geometric data of the pair are the metric $g$ and the weight $\alpha$; equivalently, Riemannian volume and the measure $\mu=e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g$. Ordinary Laplace–Beltrami operators are the case $\alpha=0$.

The spectrum alone does not determine $(g,\alpha)$. What does determine them, in the large, is the multiplication table of eigenfunctions
$$\Phi(u,v,w)=\int_X uvw\,d\mu$$
together with the list of eigenvalues \cite{Sch24,Sch26}. That statement consumes every eigenspace. The purpose of this article is a finite substitute.
Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$ and let
$$\mathcal{V}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda}\ker(P-\lambda)$$
be the corresponding spectral window. Products of window functions need not lie in $\mathcal{V}$. Write $m(u,v)=\Pi_{\Lambda'}(uv)$ for the projection onto modes up to $\Lambda'$, and
$$R(u,v)=-P\bigl(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'}\bigr)(uv)
=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda$$
for the weighted tail of those products. Throughout, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. The uniform size of the tail is
$$\Delta=\sup_{\lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1}\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2.$$

The exact subclass is $R=0$ at a finite $\Lambda'$, which is an algebraic constraint on the window, not an asymptotic one. A Wyman bound on window triples guarantees that the coefficients of $R$ exist \cite{Wym}.

The visible packet is
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\ \Lambda',\ \sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\ \Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'},\ R\bigr).$$

The cometric $\Gamma$ of $g$ is not an extra input. The identity
$$P(uv)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-2\Gamma(u,v)$$
computes $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ from $(P,\Phi)$ with remainder $R$. Two nondegeneracy hypotheses close the packet: evaluation $X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast$ is a smooth embedding, and $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T^*X$. No Weyl law is assumed, no potential is present, and no curvature bound is used.
If $R=0$, the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. If $\Delta$ is smaller than a threshold depending on the embedding of $X$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$, the same packet determines a submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ on which the metric and weight errors are bounded by $C(d,k)\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$, with $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$. When the tail of $\Phi$ occupies a finite slot, Sobolev embedding on $\Sigma$ converts this to a linear bound in $\Delta$ whose constants depend only on window dimensions and $k$. In both regimes the manifold is recovered as the space of multiplicative characters of the window, the metric by dualizing $\Gamma$, and the weight from the ratio of $\mu$ to Riemannian volume.

The theorem is recognition, not construction: $P$ is given as Witten, and the packet is asked to recover the geometry that produced it. It is not a spectral uniqueness theorem. Two Witten operators with the same spectrum, or even the same $\sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda']$, need not be isometric; the missing data are $\Phi$ and $R$. Two operators with the same exact packet ($R=0$) are isometric, with $\alpha$ agreeing up to a constant. Two operators with the same approximate packet need not be isometric; they are $C^k$-close to isometric on the scale of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$.

A distinguished slice is the unweighted first window on the sphere. There one may take $\Lambda=n$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, and $R=0$ already at $\Lambda'=2n+2$: products of linear coordinates on $S^n$ occupy only the constant slot and the quadratic harmonics. Theorems 1 and 2 then specialize to an exact recognition of the unit round sphere from that frame, and to a linear $C^0$ stability statement when the same frame is allowed small deficits. Those two statements are compared with Obata’s theorem \cite{Oba62} and with quantitative Obata theorems on the Ricci side \cite{CMS}. They are not Ricci theorems. They use a linear frame and its products, not a lower bound on Ricci curvature and a single first eigenfunction.
The article is organized as follows. Section 2 fixes the Witten category and the window packet. Section 3 extracts $\Gamma$ from the multiplication rule with remainder $R$. Section 4 identifies points of $X$ with characters of the window. Section 5 dualizes $\Gamma$ to a metric and reads $\alpha$ from the two measures. Section 6 assembles Theorems 1 and 2. Section 7 specializes to the unweighted first window on the sphere. Section 8 records what the packet does not determine.

Geometry sits in the multiplication table, not in the list of eigenvalues. The contribution here is that a finite, explicitly tailed piece of that table already reconstructs a Witten pair, and that the unit sphere is the minimal unweighted instance of the same mechanism.

\subsection{Results}
Standing hypotheses: $X$ closed and connected, $P=P_\alpha$ a Witten operator \cite{Wit82}, packet as in §2, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$, Wyman on window triples \cite{Wym}, W1, W2.

\begin{theorem}[exact recognition]
\label{thm-1}
Assume $R=0$. The packet
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\Lambda',\sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'}\bigr)$$
determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. Evaluation is a Riemannian isometry of $(X,g)$ onto the character space of $(\mathcal{V},m)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$, and $P$ is the Witten operator of the reconstructed pair.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[approximate recognition]
\label{thm-2}
Let $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$. There exists $\Delta_*>0$ such that if $\Delta<\Delta_*$, evaluation produces a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\iota:X\to\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ with
$$\bigl\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
+\bigl\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}.$$
If the tail of $\Phi$ that defines $R$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$, then $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}\le C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\,\Delta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[round sphere, exact window]
\label{thm-a}
Assume $\alpha=0$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, $\Lambda=n$, and $R=0$ at $\Lambda'=2n+2$. Suppose there is a frame $x_0,\dots,x_n$ in $\mathcal{V}$ with $Px_a=nx_a$, $\int x_ax_b=\delta_{ab}$, $\int x_a=0$, $\sum_ax_a^2=1$, each $x_ax_b-\delta_{ab}/(n+1)$ an eigenfunction at $2n+2$, and $\{dx_a\}$ spanning $T^*X$. Then $X$ is isometric to the unit round sphere, $P=-\Delta$, and $\operatorname{span}\{x_a\}=H_1$. Scaling: the pair $(\lambda,2\lambda+2\lambda/n)$ gives radius $\sqrt{n/\lambda}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[round sphere, quantitative window]
In the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm-a}, allow deficits
$$\delta_1=\Bigl\lVert\sum_ax_a^2-1\Bigr\rVert_{C^0},\quad
\delta_3=\text{rank gap of }\Gamma(x_a,x_b),\quad
\delta_\lambda=\lvert\lambda-n\rvert,$$
$$\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}=\sup_{a,b}\lVert R(x_a,x_b)\rVert_2,\qquad
\delta=\delta_1+\delta_3+\delta_\lambda+\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}.$$
If $\delta<\delta_*(n)$, then $X$ is a $C^1$-graph over $S^n$ and
$$d_{\mathrm{GH}}(X,S^n)+d_{C^0}(g,g_{\mathrm{rd}})\le C(n)\,\delta.$$
\end{theorem}

This is linear stability of Theorem~\ref{thm-a}. It does not use Ricci curvature \cite{Oba62,CMS}.

\section{The Packet}
Let $X$ be a closed connected smooth manifold and let
$$P=P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$$
act in $L^2(X,\mu)$ with $d\mu=e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g$ \cite{Wit82}. Assume $P$ is essentially self-adjoint, with discrete spectrum
$$0=\lambda_0<\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\cdots\to+\infty$$
and $\ker P=\operatorname{span}\{1\}$. The principal symbol of $P$ is the cometric $\Gamma$ of $g$. For all smooth $u,v$,

\begin{equation}
P(uv)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-2\Gamma(u,v).\tag{A1}
\end{equation}

Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$. Write
$$\mathcal{V}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda}\ker(P-\lambda),\qquad
\mathcal{V}'=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda'}\ker(P-\lambda),$$
and let $\Pi_{\Lambda'}$ be the orthogonal projection onto $\mathcal{V}'$. Throughout, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. Set
$$m(u,v)=\Pi_{\Lambda'}(uv),\qquad
\Phi(u,v,w)=\int_X uvw\,d\mu.$$

The weighted tail of the product is
$$R(u,v)=-P\bigl(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'}\bigr)(uv)
=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda,$$
and
$$\Delta=\sup\bigl\{\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2:\ u,v\in\mathcal{V},\ \lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1\bigr\}.$$

The exact subclass is $R=0$, equivalently $\Delta=0$.

Wyman. There exist $\delta\ge 0$ and $C<\infty$ such that for all $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$ and every eigenfunction $w$,
$$\bigl\lvert\Phi(u,v,w)\bigr\rvert
\le C\,(1+\lambda_u+\lambda_v+\lambda_w)^\delta\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2\lVert w\rVert_2$$
\cite{Wym}.

W1. Evaluation $\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast$, $\iota(x)(u)=u(x)$, is a smooth embedding onto a closed submanifold of $\mathcal{V}^\ast$. The same holds for evaluation $X\to(\mathcal{V}')^\ast$. Characters are therefore evaluations on $\mathcal{V}'$, and the comparison error $E$ of §4 vanishes.


W2. The differentials $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ span $T^*X$ at every point.

The visible packet is
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\ \Lambda',\ \sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\ \Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'},\ R\bigr).$$
On pairs from $\mathcal{V}$, (A1) reads
$$2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)+R(u,v).$$
Thus $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ is not an independent input. No Weyl law, no potential, and no curvature bound is assumed. Dimension of $X$ is the rank in W2.



\section{The Cometric From the Window}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lma-1}
Let $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. Then
$$2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)+R(u,v),$$
where
$$R(u,v)=-P\bigl(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'}\bigr)(uv)
=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda.$$
In particular $\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2\le\Delta\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2$. If $R=0$, then $\Gamma(u,v)$ is determined by $(P,\Phi)$. The bilinear map $\Gamma:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to C^\infty(X)$ does not depend on the orthonormal basis used to expand $\Phi$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
 Identity (A1) gives
$$2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P(uv).$$
Split $uv=m(u,v)+t$ with $t=(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)$. Then $P(uv)=P(m(u,v))+Pt$ and $R=-Pt$. If $R=0$, then $t=0$, so $m(u,v)=uv$ and every coefficient of $uv$ against $\mathcal{V}'$ is a value of $\Phi$. Basis-independence: $P$ and $\Phi$ are defined on $\mathcal{V}$ without choices, and $m$ is orthogonal projection of the pointwise product. Changing orthonormal bases of $\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{V}'$ conjugates the array of $\Phi$ and leaves $\Gamma(u,v)$ unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Sogge and $\lVert t\rVert_{C^0}$]
Hörmander–Sogge gives $\lVert e_\lambda\rVert_{C^0}\le C_S(n)\,(1+\lambda)^{(n-1)/4}$ \cite{Sog88}. Expanding $t$ and applying Cauchy–Schwarz with the weight in $R=Pt$ yields, for a tail supported in a finite slot $\Lambda<\lambda\le\Lambda''$,
$$\lVert t\rVert_{C^0}\le C(n,\Lambda',\Lambda'')\,\Delta,$$
and therefore
$$\lVert R\rVert_{C^0(X)}\le C(n,\Lambda',\Lambda'')\,\Delta.$$
Wyman bounds the coefficients of $R$ \cite{Wym}; Sogge bounds the modes. Neither is a curvature comparison.]
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
 If the $\Phi$-tail that defines $R$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$, all norms on that slot are equivalent and
$$\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(X)}\le C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\,\Delta.$$
If the tail is infinite, $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ remains the modulus.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
 If $R=0$, the Gram functions $\Gamma(u,v)$ for $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$ are packet data. W2 is then a nondegeneracy condition on those functions. If $\Delta>0$, the same Gram functions are determined up to an $L^2$ error of size $\Delta$, and up to a $C^k$ error of size $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ after the comparison of §4.
\end{corollary}

\section{Characters}
Let $m:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to\mathcal{V}'$ be the packet product of §2. By W1, evaluation
$$\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast,\qquad
\iota':X\to(\mathcal{V}')^\ast$$
are smooth embeddings onto closed submanifolds. Characters of the window are evaluations on $\mathcal{V}'$. In particular the comparison error between $R$ as a function on $X$ and $R$ as a function on the image $\Sigma=\iota(X)$ vanishes: $R_\Sigma\circ\iota=R_X$.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lma-2}
There exists $\Delta_*=\Delta_*(d,\iota(X))>0$, with $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$, such that if $\Delta<\Delta_*$, then:

$\iota(X)$ is a $C^\infty$ embedded submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$;

$\iota:X\to\Sigma$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism, and a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism if $R=0$;

for every $k\ge 0$,$$c(k)^{-1}\,\lVert R_X\rVert_{C^k(X)}
\le\lVert R_\Sigma\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}
\le c(k)\,\lVert R_X\rVert_{C^k(X)},$$with $c(k)=C(d,k)$.

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
 W1 says $\iota$ is already a smooth embedding onto a closed submanifold, so the first item is W1 and does not use $\Delta$. The product map $F(\xi;u,v)=\xi(m(u,v))-\xi(u)\xi(v)$ vanishes on $\iota(X)$ up to the tail $t$. Because evaluation extends to $\mathcal{V}'$, one has $\xi(m(u,v))=m(u,v)(x)$ at $\xi=\iota(x)$, and the only defect is $t(x)$. Differentiating $F$ in the $\mathcal{V}^\ast$-directions at $\iota(x)$ yields the family $(u,v)\mapsto\xi(u)\,dv+\xi(v)\,du$ on $T_x^*X$. W2 says $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T_x^*X$, so this family is transverse to $\iota(X)$. Persistence of transverse embeddings for $\Delta<\Delta_*$ keeps $\iota$ a $C^1$ diffeomorphism onto its image. At $R=0$ one has $t=0$, $F$ vanishes exactly on $\iota(X)$, and $\iota$ is $C^\infty$. The $C^k$ comparison of $R_X$ and $R_\Sigma$ is the chain rule for $\iota$ and $\iota^{-1}$, whose $C^{k-1}$ norms stay bounded for $\Delta<\Delta_*$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $R=0$, $X$ is identified with the character space of the finite algebra $(\mathcal{V},m)$ as a $C^\infty$ embedded submanifold of $\mathcal{V}^\ast$. If $\Delta<\Delta_*$, the same identification holds as a $C^1$ diffeomorphism, and $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ may be used interchangeably with $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(X)}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Residual ambiguity is an orthogonal change of basis of $\mathcal{V}$ preserving $\Phi$, a compact subgroup of $O(d)$. The threshold $\Delta_*$ depends on $d$ and on the $C^1$ geometry of $\iota(X)$ at $\Delta=0$. It is not universal in $n$ except on the spherical window of §7.
\end{remark}

\section{Metric and Weight}
\begin{lemma}Assume Lemma~\ref{lma-1} and W2. There is a unique Riemannian metric $g$ on $X$ such that
$$\Gamma(u,v)=g^{-1}(du,dv)\qquad\text{for all }u,v\in\mathcal{V}.$$
This $g$ is the metric whose cometric is the principal symbol of $P$. After Lemma 9,
$$\bigl\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}.$$
If the tail of $\Phi$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$, the right-hand side is at most $C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\,\Delta$.
\label{lma-3}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
W2 says $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T^*X$. The assignment $(du,dv)\mapsto\Gamma(u,v)$ is well-defined on that spanning set: $\Gamma(u,u)=\sigma_P(du,du)$, so $du=0$ implies $\Gamma(u,\cdot)=0$. Positive-definiteness is the assumption that the principal symbol of $P$ is an inner product. Dualizing gives $g$. In a local frame $\{u_a\}\subset\mathcal{V}$ of rank $n=\dim X$,
$$g^{ab}=\Gamma(u_a,u_b)$$
on $\operatorname{span}\{du_a\}$, and $g$ is the inverse matrix. Independence of the frame is change of basis for a cometric. The $C^k$ estimate is Lemma 5 plus the comparison of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ on $X$ and on $\Sigma$ from Lemma 9. Finite-tail conversion is the remark in §3.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
Let $g$ be the metric of Lemma 12 and let $\mu$ be the measure determined by
$$\int uv\,d\mu=\Phi(1,u,v),\qquad u,v\in\mathcal{V},$$
extended by continuity to $L^2(\mu)$. Then $\mu$ is a smooth positive multiple of $\mathrm{vol}_g$. The function
$$\alpha=-\log\frac{d\mu}{d\mathrm{vol}_g}$$
is smooth, unique up to an additive constant, and the given operator $P$ is the Witten operator of $(g,\alpha)$. If $\Delta<\Delta_*$,
$$\bigl\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}.$$
\label{lma-4}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
 By the standing category, $P$ is already the Witten operator of some pair $(g_0,\alpha_0)$. Lemma 12 identifies $g=g_0$, because both metrics have the same principal symbol. The inner product that makes $P$ self-adjoint is $\mu=e^{-\alpha_0}\mathrm{vol}_{g_0}$. On the other hand $\Phi(1,\cdot,\cdot)$ is that inner product, since $1=\ker P$ and $\Phi(1,u,v)=\langle u,v\rangle_{L^2(\mu)}$. Thus $\mu=e^{-\alpha_0}\mathrm{vol}_g$, so $\alpha=\alpha_0+c$. A Witten operator on a closed manifold is determined by $(g,\alpha)$ up to that constant. The $C^k$ estimate follows from Lemma 12 and the elliptic regularity of $\log(d\mu/d\mathrm{vol}_g)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark} Lemma~\ref{lma-4} is recognition, not construction: it uses that $P$ was Witten. The two measures $\mu$ and $\mathrm{vol}_g$ are the only source of $\alpha$. The packet does not store $\alpha$ separately.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
 Combining Lemmas 9, 12, and 13, the packet determines a Riemannian manifold $(\Sigma,g_\Sigma)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$ and a weight $\alpha_\Sigma$ on $\Sigma$. Evaluation pulls these back to $(g,\alpha)$ exactly when $R=0$, and to within $C(d,k)\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ when $\Delta<\Delta_*$.
\end{corollary}


\section{Recognition}
The lemmas of §§3–5 assemble into Theorem~\ref{thm-1} and Theorem~\ref{thm-2}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-1}]
Assume $R=0$. Lemma 5 gives $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ as a function of $(P,\Phi)$, with remainder zero. Lemma 9 identifies $X$ with the character space $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ of the finite algebra $(\mathcal{V},m)$ by a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism $\iota$. Lemma 12 dualizes $\Gamma$ to a Riemannian metric $g_\Sigma$ on $\Sigma$ with $\iota^*g_\Sigma=g$. Lemma 13 reads $\alpha$ from $\mu/\mathrm{vol}_g$, up to an additive constant, and identifies $P$ with the Witten operator of that pair. Thus $\iota$ is a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry, and the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to that isometry and the constant on $\alpha$. Residual freedom is an orthogonal change of basis of $\mathcal{V}$ preserving $\Phi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-2}]
Assume $\Delta<\Delta_*$, with $\Delta_*$ as in Lemma 9. Lemma 5 gives $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ with remainder $R$. Lemma 9 produces a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\iota:X\to\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ and identifies $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ with $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(X)}$ up to $C(d,k)$. Lemma 12 upgrades the cometric error to
$$\bigl\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\bigr\rVert_{C^k}\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}.$$
Lemma 13 gives the same bound for $\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c$. If the tail of $\Phi$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$, the remarks of §3 convert $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ into $C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\Delta$. Hence $\iota$ is a $C^k$ almost-isometry, not an isometry, unless $R=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
 wo packets for the same operator at different admissible cutoffs produce isometric copies if both have $R=0$, and almost-isometric copies on the scale of the two values of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ otherwise. The theorems do not identify merely isospectral Witten operators.
\end{remark}

\section{The Unweighted First Window on the Sphere}
This section is Theorems 1 and 2 on a single window: $\alpha=0$, $\Lambda=n$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, and products of the window supported in slots $\{0,2n+2\}$ when $R=0$.
Let $\{x_a\}_{a=0}^n$ be an $L^2$-orthonormal frame in $\mathcal{V}$ as in Theorem 3, and write $F=(x_0,\dots,x_n):X\to\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$.

\begin{lemma} Assume the hypotheses of Theorem 3. Then $\sum_a x_a^2=1$, $\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$, and $dF$ has rank $n$ at every point. Consequently $F(X)$ is a closed embedded hypersurface in the unit sphere of $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The quadric $\sum_a x_a^2=1$ is given. Differentiating and using $Px_a=nx_a$ with (A1) yields
$$2\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=n x_ax_b+n x_bx_a-P(x_ax_b).$$
By hypothesis $x_ax_b-\delta_{ab}/(n+1)$ is an eigenfunction at $2n+2$, and $P(1)=0$, so
$$P(x_ax_b)=(2n+2)\Bigl(x_ax_b-\frac{\delta_{ab}}{n+1}\Bigr).$$
Hence $\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$. The matrix $\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$ has rank $n$ on the unit sphere, and $\{dx_a\}$ spans $T^*X$ by hypothesis, which is W4 for this frame. W2 follows: $F$ is an immersion, $X$ is closed, and $F(X)$ lies on $S^n$, so $F$ is a covering onto its image. Simple connectedness of $S^n$ for $n\ge 2$, or a direct check for $n=1$, makes $F$ a diffeomorphism onto $S^n$.
\end{proof}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-a}] Lemma 15 and Theorem 1 give that $X$ is isometric to the unit round sphere. The metric dual to $\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$ is the round metric of radius $1$, and $\alpha=0$ because $\mu=\mathrm{vol}_g$. Thus $P=-\Delta$. The frame spans the first harmonics because it is an $(n+1)$-dimensional eigenspace at eigenvalue $n$. If the first eigenvalue is $\lambda$ rather than $n$, the same identities with the pair $(\lambda,2\lambda+2\lambda/n)$ rescale the radius to $\sqrt{n/\lambda}$. $\square$
\end{proof}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-b}]
 Work in the setting of Theorem 3 with deficits $\delta_1,\delta_3,\delta_\lambda$ and
$$\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}=\sup_{a,b}\lVert R(x_a,x_b)\rVert_2,$$
and set $\delta=\delta_1+\delta_3+\delta_\lambda+\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}$. Then $\sum_a x_a^2=1+O_{C^0}(\delta_1)$, so $F$ lands in a $C^0$-neighborhood of $S^n$. The remainder $\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}$ is $R$ on this frame. The exact support is the slot $2n+2$; any mass in $R$ occupies a finite cluster, so $d_{\mathrm{tail}}=O(n^2)$ and the remarks of §3 give
$$\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}\le C(n,k)\,\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}.$$
Lemma 5 yields
$$\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b+E_{ab},\qquad
\lVert E_{ab}\rVert_{C^0}\le C(n)\,\delta.$$
The rank gap $\delta_3$ and $\delta_\lambda$ keep $dF$ of rank $n$ for $\delta<\delta_*(n)$. Lemma 9 therefore produces a $C^1$ diffeomorphism from $X$ onto a graph over $S^n$. Theorem 2, with constants reduced to $C(n)$ by the fixed window $d=n+1$, yields
$$d_{\mathrm{GH}}(X,S^n)+d_{C^0}(g,g_{\mathrm{rd}})\le C(n)\,\delta.$$
No Ricci bound is used. Diameter control follows from the diffeomorphism onto a graph over $S^n$, not from Cheng comparison.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Obata characterizes $S^n$ by $\operatorname{Ric}\ge(n-1)g$ and $\lambda_1=n$, using one eigenfunction \cite{Oba62}. Theorems 3 and 4 use an $(n+1)$-frame and its products. Quantitative Obata is Ricci-side stability of Obata \cite{CMS}. Theorem 4 is frame-side stability of Theorem 3. They are not the same theorem.
\end{remark}

\section{What the Packet Does Not Determine}
The packet of §2 is a finite piece of the multiplication table of $P$. It does not determine the rest of $\sigma(P)$. Two Witten operators with the same window products and different high eigenvalues are not distinguished.

It does not determine a potential. The standing category is Witten: $V=0$. A Schrödinger term is a different operator and a different paper \cite{Sch26}.

It does not yield spectral uniqueness. Two operators with the same spectrum, or the same $\sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda']$, need not be isometric; the missing data are $\Phi$ and $R$. Two operators with the same exact packet ($R=0$) are isometric. Two operators with the same approximate packet need not be; they are $C^k$-close on the scale of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$.

It does not yield a constant depending only on $n$ in Theorem 2. The factor $C(d,k)$ depends on the window dimension. A universal $C(n)$ appears only in Theorem 4, where $d=n+1$ is fixed.

It does not use Ricci curvature, a Weyl law, or the full table $\Phi$ on $\bigoplus_n\ker(P-\lambda_n)$. Those are the inputs of Obata theorems \cite{Oba62,CMS}, of eigenvalue asymptotics, and of the global comparison theorem \cite{Sch24,Sch26}, respectively. The present arguments use a window frame and its products.

Two packets for the same operator at different admissible cutoffs are consistent with Theorems 1 and 2. That consistency is not part of one packet; each packet has its own $(\Lambda,\Lambda',R)$.

The contribution of the paper is the converse direction inside this class: a finite, explicitly tailed window already reconstructs a Witten pair, and the unit round sphere is the minimal unweighted instance of that reconstruction.

